 {
  "slug": "clinical-u3-stroke-watershed",
  "kind": "clinical",
  "topic": "clinical-u3",
  "status": "live",
  "position": 6,
  "source": {
   "type": "clinical",
   "ref": "lecture-18 CNS pathology"
  },
  "vignette": "After cardiac arrest a patient has weakness of both shoulders and upper arms with preserved hands and legs ('man in a barrel').",
  "prompt": "Which kind of stroke?",
  "answer": "Bilateral watershed infarcts",
  "accept": [
   "border-zone infarcts",
   "watershed",
   "ACA-MCA watershed",
   "MCA-PCA watershed",
   "bilateral border-zone infarcts"
  ],
  "require_side": false,
  "format": "keywords",
  "keywords": [
   [
    "watershed",
    "border"
   ]
  ],
  "tags": [
   "clinical",
   "stroke",
   "unit:u3",
   "kind:clinical",
   "source:lecture",
   "theory:lesion"
  ],
  "structures": [],
  "explain": "Border zones between the MCA and ACA or PCA territories are the last to receive blood when perfusion falls globally, as in cardiac arrest. Shoulder and upper arm cortex lies in the ACA-MCA watershed, so both proximal arms weaken while hands and legs are spared ('man in a barrel'). Border zones between the MCA and ACA or PCA territories are the last to receive blood when perfusion falls globally, as in cardiac arrest. The course slide (Lecture 18) shows the MCA-PCA border zone as the most common type; the 'man in a barrel' pattern (both proximal arms weak, hands and legs spared) is classically the ACA-MCA watershed."
 },
